OAI_PAPER({"slug": "ample-rank-two-bundles-on-the-quadric-surface-without-griffiths-positive-metrics-September-24-2026", "title": "Ample rank-two bundles on the quadric surface without Griffiths-positive metrics", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/ample-rank-two-bundles-on-the-quadric-surface-without-griffiths-positive-metrics-September-24-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "050", "family_title": "A counterexample to Griffiths’ positivity conjecture", "abstract_html": "We give counterexamples to Griffiths' conjecture in rank two on the quadric surface \\(\\mathbb P^1\\times\\mathbb P^1\\). We construct an explicit bundle <i>G</i> whose coordinatewise power pullbacks, tensored with \\(\\mathcal O(1,1)\\), are ample for every positive power, but admit no smooth strictly Griffiths-positive Hermitian metric for all sufficiently large powers.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem \\(1.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  S=\\mathbf P^1\\times\\mathbf P^1,\\qquad P=\\mathcal O_S(1,1),\n  \\]\n  with projections \\(p_1,p_2:S\\to\\mathbf P^1\\), and write\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal O_S(r,s)=p_1^*\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P^1}(r)\\otimes p_2^*\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P^1}(s).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For each positive integer \\(m\\), define\n  \\[\n  f_m([z_0:z_1],[w_0:w_1])\n  =([z_0^m:z_1^m],[w_0^m:w_1^m]).\n  \\]\n  This is finite of degree \\(m^2\\), and\n  \\[\n  f_m^*\\mathcal O_S(r,s)\\simeq\\mathcal O_S(mr,ms),\n  \\qquad f_m^*P\\simeq P^{\\otimes m}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a vector bundle \\(V\\), \\(\\mathbf P(V)\\) parametrizes one-dimensional quotients, and \\(\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P(V)}(1)\\) is its tautological quotient line bundle. The bundle \\(V\\) is ample when \\(\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P(V)}(1)\\) is ample.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the curvature convention\n  \\[\n  \\Theta_{E,h}\n  =i\\sum_{j,k,\\alpha,\\beta}\n  c_{jk\\alpha\\beta}\\,dz_j\\wedge d\\bar z_k\\otimes e_\\alpha^*\\otimes e_\\beta\n  \\]\n  in a frame orthonormal at the point under consideration. Strict Griffiths positivity means\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{j,k,\\alpha,\\beta}\n  c_{jk\\alpha\\beta}\\xi_j\\bar\\xi_kv_\\alpha\\bar v_\\beta&gt;0\n  \\qquad(\\xi\\ne0,\\ v\\ne0).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Throughout, “psh” means plurisubharmonic, and on a complex curve\n  \\[\n  i\\partial\\bar\\partial u=iu_{z\\bar z}\\,dz\\wedge d\\bar z.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an algebraic vector bundle \\(G\\) of rank two on \\(S\\) such that, for every positive integer \\(m\\),\n\\[\nE_m=f_m^*G\\otimes P\n\\]\nis ample. There is an integer \\(m_0\\) such that, for every \\(m\\ge m_0\\), \\(E_m\\) admits no smooth Hermitian metric with strictly Griffiths-positive Chern curvature.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take the algebraic rank-two bundle \\(G\\) of Proposition \\(1\\). By Proposition \\(2\\), every\n\\[\nE_m=f_m^*G\\otimes P\n\\]\nis ample in the quotient convention.</p><p>If the set of positive integers \\(m\\) for which \\(E_m\\) has a smooth strictly Griffiths-positive metric were infinite, it would contain a sequence tending to infinity. Proposition \\(3\\) would then give a finite, nonnegative, nonzero psh function on the total space of \\(G^\\vee\\) that is a positive semidefinite Hermitian quadratic form on every fiber. Proposition \\(4\\) excludes precisely such a function.</p><p>Consequently, that set of integers is finite. Choose \\(m_0\\) larger than all its elements, taking \\(m_0=1\\) if the set is empty. Every \\(m\\ge m_0\\) gives the required counterexample.</p><p>The limit in the proof is permitted to lose rank, but not to vanish identically or to lose its quadratic structure at any base point. These are exactly the properties needed to transfer the graph relations to a nonzero invariant measure. The argument requires no uniform estimates for the input metrics or their curvature, and it does not determine an effective value of \\(m_0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Proposition \\(2.3\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S=\\mathbf P^1\\times\\mathbf P^1\\), and identify the projective line used below with the lines in \\(\\mathbf C^2\\), whose tautological subbundle is \\(\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P^1}(-1)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  a=e^{i\\pi/3},\\qquad\n  s=\\frac12-\\frac{i(1+2\\sqrt2)}{2\\sqrt3},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  A=\\begin{pmatrix}a&amp;0\\\\0&amp;a^{-1}\\end{pmatrix},\n  \\qquad\n  B=\\begin{pmatrix}s&amp;1\\\\ \\sqrt2/3&amp;1-s\\end{pmatrix}.\n  \\]\n  Use the same letters for the induced automorphisms of \\(\\mathbf P^1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(M\\in\\{I,A,B\\}\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\Gamma_M=\\{(x,Mx):x\\in\\mathbf P^1\\},\n  \\qquad\n  D=\\Gamma_I\\cup\\Gamma_A\\cup\\Gamma_B\n  \\]\n  with the reduced structure.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  b=24,\\qquad\n  L_1=p_1^*\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P^1}(b),\\qquad\n  L_2=p_2^*\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P^1}(b).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Along \\(\\Gamma_M\\), the action of \\(M\\) on tautological lines induces, by taking its \\(b\\)-th tensor power, an isomorphism from \\(L_1^{-1}\\) to \\(L_2^{-1}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These three isomorphisms glue to an algebraic isomorphism\n\\[\n\\alpha:L_1^{-1}|_D\\xrightarrow{\\sim}L_2^{-1}|_D,\n\\qquad\n\\alpha|_{\\Gamma_M}=M^{\\otimes b}.\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\n\\beta=(\\alpha^\\vee)^{-1}:L_1|_D\\longrightarrow L_2|_D\n\\]\nand define\n\\[\nG=\\{(u_1,u_2)\\in L_1\\oplus L_2:\nu_2|_D=\\beta(u_1|_D)\\}.\n\\]\nThen \\(G\\) is an algebraic vector bundle of rank two. Both projections\n\\(\\rho_i:G\\to L_i\\) are surjective, with exact sequences\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow L_i(-D)\\longrightarrow G\n\\xrightarrow{\\rho_j}L_j\\longrightarrow0,\n\\qquad \\{i,j\\}=\\{1,2\\}.\n\\]\nFor \\(F=G^\\vee\\), the dual inclusions\n\\[\n\\iota_i=\\rho_i^\\vee:L_i^{-1}\\hookrightarrow F\n\\]\nspan each fiber over \\(S\\setminus D\\) and satisfy\n\\[\n\\iota_2\\circ\\alpha=\\iota_1\\qquad\\text{on }D.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The bundle is an elementary transformation of \\(L_1\\oplus L_2\\) along the effective Cartier divisor \\(D\\). We verify the gluing and the local basis directly, since the resulting quotient maps are needed in both subsequent arguments.</p><p>At a point of \\(\\Gamma_M\\cap\\Gamma_N\\), let \\(v\\) span the first-coordinate line. For some eigenvalue \\(\\lambda\\) of \\(M^{-1}N\\),\n\\[\nNv=\\lambda Mv.\n\\]\nBy Lemma \\(1.1\\), \\(\\lambda^{24}=1\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n(Nv)^{\\otimes b}=(Mv)^{\\otimes b}.\n\\]\nThus the two maps agree as maps of the actual line fibers. This also covers the reversed pair, for which the eigenvalue is \\(\\lambda^{-1}\\).</p><p>We check descent at a node. If \\(R\\) is the local ring of \\(S\\) there and \\((x),(y)\\) are the branch ideals, Lemma \\(1.2\\) gives transversality, hence\n\\[\n(x)\\cap(y)=(xy)\n\\]\nand the exact sequence\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow R/(xy)\n\\longrightarrow R/(x)\\oplus R/(y)\n\\longrightarrow R/(x,y)\\longrightarrow0,\n\\]\nwhose last map is the difference of the residue values. Tensoring this sequence by the locally free rank-one sheaf\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}(L_1^{-1},L_2^{-1})|_D\n\\]\nshows that the equal fiber values are precisely the compatibility needed to descend the two branch sections. Their common value is nonzero, so the descended scalar is a unit in the nodal local ring. The component maps therefore glue to \\(\\alpha\\).</p><p>Near a point of \\(D\\), choose frames of \\(L_1,L_2\\) and a local equation \\(d\\) of \\(D\\). The scalar defining \\(\\beta\\) modulo \\(d\\) lifts, after shrinking, to a regular unit \\(h\\). In these frames the module defining \\(G\\) has basis\n\\[\ne=(1,h),\\qquad f=(0,d).\n\\]\nIndeed, every element has a unique expression \\(u_1e+kf\\), since \\(u_2-hu_1\\) is divisible by the non-zero-divisor \\(d\\). Away from \\(D\\), the sheaf is simply \\(L_1\\oplus L_2\\), proving local freeness everywhere.</p><p>The first projection maps \\(e\\) to \\(1\\), and the second maps \\(e\\) to the unit \\(h\\), so both are surjective. Setting the \\(j\\)-th coordinate equal to zero in the definition of \\(G\\) identifies its kernel with \\(L_i(-D)\\), proving the asserted exact sequences.</p><p>Their duals are line subbundles of \\(F\\), and away from \\(D\\) they span. On \\(D\\), if \\(v\\in L_1^{-1}\\) and \\((u_1,u_2)\\in G\\), then\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\iota_2(\\alpha v)\\bigr)(u_1,u_2)\n=(\\alpha v)(\\beta u_1)\n=v(u_1)\n=\\bigl(\\iota_1(v)\\bigr)(u_1,u_2),\n\\]\nbecause \\(\\alpha^\\vee\\beta=\\mathrm{Id}\\). Thus \\(\\iota_2\\alpha=\\iota_1\\) on \\(D\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.1\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(A,B\\) be the matrices defined in Proposition \\(1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The matrices \\(A,B\\) belong to \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbf C)\\) and satisfy\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}A=\\operatorname{tr}B=1,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{tr}(A^{-1}B)=-\\sqrt2,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{tr}(AB)=1+\\sqrt2.\n\\]\nThe orders of \\(A,B,A^{-1}B\\) in \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbf C)\\) are \\(6,6,8\\), respectively. The induced projective orders of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are both \\(3\\). The matrix \\(AB\\) has two positive real reciprocal eigenvalues of distinct moduli, and \\(A,B\\) have no common eigenline.</p>", "proof": "<p>The value of \\(s\\) gives\n\\[\ns(1-s)=1+\\frac{\\sqrt2}{3},\n\\qquad\na^{-1}s+a(1-s)=-\\sqrt2.\n\\]\nThese identities prove the determinant and trace assertions except for the trace of \\(AB\\).</p><p>The characteristic polynomial of each of \\(A,B\\) is \\(t^2-t+1\\), with roots \\(e^{\\pm i\\pi/3}\\), while that of \\(A^{-1}B\\) is\n\\[\nt^2+\\sqrt2\\,t+1,\n\\]\nwith roots \\(e^{\\pm3i\\pi/4}\\). The roots in each pair are distinct, proving the claimed orders; in particular,\n\\[\nA^3=B^3=-\\mathrm{Id}.\n\\]</p><p>Cayley–Hamilton gives \\(A+A^{-1}=\\mathrm{Id}\\). Multiplication by \\(B\\) and taking traces therefore yield\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(AB)\n=\\operatorname{tr}B-\\operatorname{tr}(A^{-1}B)\n=1+\\sqrt2&gt;2.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\det(AB)=1\\), its eigenvalues are positive real reciprocals, one greater than one.</p><p>Finally, the eigenlines of \\(A\\) are the two coordinate lines, and neither is preserved by \\(B\\), whose two off-diagonal entries are nonzero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(\\Gamma_I,\\Gamma_A,\\Gamma_B\\) and their reduced union \\(D\\) be as in Proposition \\(1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The divisor \\(D\\) has bidegree \\((3,3)\\) and exactly six singular points, all ordinary transverse intersections of two components. There are no triple intersections.</p>", "proof": "<p>Each graph is a smooth divisor of bidegree \\((1,1)\\). An intersection of \\(\\Gamma_M\\) and \\(\\Gamma_N\\) corresponds to a fixed point of \\(M^{-1}N\\), hence to an eigenline of that matrix. The relative matrices are \\(A,B,A^{-1}B\\) or their inverses.</p><p>By Lemma \\(1.1\\), each relative matrix has two distinct eigenvalues, and the multiplier at either projective fixed point is the ratio of those eigenvalues in one order or the other. This ratio is not one. The fixed points are therefore simple, so each pair of graphs meets transversely at two points.</p><p>A point on all three graphs would give a common eigenline of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), contrary to Lemma \\(1.1\\). Thus the six pairwise intersections are distinct. Their local equations are products of two regular parameters, and the asserted description follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition \\(3.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be the rank-two bundle constructed in Proposition \\(1\\), with\n  \\[\n  L_1=\\mathcal O_S(24,0),\\qquad\n  L_2=\\mathcal O_S(0,24),\\qquad\n  \\mathcal O_S(D)=\\mathcal O_S(3,3),\n  \\]\n  and the two exact sequences\n  \\[\n  0\\longrightarrow L_i(-D)\\longrightarrow G\\longrightarrow L_j\\longrightarrow0,\n  \\qquad\\{i,j\\}=\\{1,2\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a positive integer \\(m\\), put\n  \\[\n  E_m=f_m^*G\\otimes P,\n  \\]\n  where \\(P=\\mathcal O_S(1,1)\\) and \\(f_m\\) is the coordinatewise power map.</p><p class=\"li\">Powers of line bundles in this proposition mean tensor powers.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every positive integer \\(m\\), the bundle \\(E_m\\) is ample. More precisely,\n\\[\n\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P(E_m)}(n)\n\\]\nis very ample for every even integer \\(n\\) satisfying\n\\[\nn-1\\ge3m.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For each positive integer \\(m\\), Lemmas \\(2.1\\) and \\(2.2\\) give global generation of\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Sym}^n(f_m^*G)\\otimes P^{n-1}\n\\]\nfor every even \\(n\\) with \\(n-1\\ge3m\\). Lemma \\(2.3\\) then gives very ampleness of\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P(f_m^*G)}(1)\\otimes\\pi^*P\\bigr)^n.\n\\]</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\mathbf P(E_m)\n=\\mathbf P(f_m^*G\\otimes P)\n\\simeq\\mathbf P(f_m^*G)\n\\]\nidentifies \\(\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P(E_m)}(1)\\) with\n\\[\n\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P(f_m^*G)}(1)\\otimes\\pi^*P.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P(E_m)}(n)\\) is very ample in the stated range. In particular, \\(E_m\\) is ample.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "This is the alternating-filtration step in the proof of original Proposition \\(3.1\\).", "statement": "<p>This is the alternating-filtration step in the proof of original Proposition \\(3.1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix \\(m\\ge1\\), and write\n\\[\nV=f_m^*G,\\qquad\n\\widetilde L_i=f_m^*L_i,\\qquad\n\\widetilde D=f_m^*D,\n\\]\n\\[\nK_i=\\widetilde L_i(-\\widetilde D),\n\\qquad\nK=K_1\\otimes K_2.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nK_1=\\mathcal O_S(21m,-3m),\\qquad\nK_2=\\mathcal O_S(-3m,21m),\n\\]\n\\[\nK=\\mathcal O_S(18m,18m),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal O_S(\\widetilde D)=\\mathcal O_S(3m,3m).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every even positive integer \\(n\\), \\(\\operatorname{Sym}^nV\\) has a filtration by subbundles whose line-bundle factors are\n\\[\nK^j\\otimes\\widetilde L_2^{\\,n-2j},\n\\qquad\nK^j\\otimes\\widetilde L_1^{\\,n-2j}(-\\widetilde D),\n\\qquad 0\\le j&lt;n/2,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nK^{n/2}.\n\\]\nThe filtration remains valid at points where the two kernel lines have the same fiber image, including nodes and nonreduced pullbacks of \\(D\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Pulling back the two quotient sequences gives\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow K_i\\longrightarrow V\n\\longrightarrow\\widetilde L_j\\longrightarrow0,\n\\qquad\\{i,j\\}=\\{1,2\\}.\n\\]\nThese sequences remain exact because their original quotients are locally free.</p><p>For an extension\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow K'\\longrightarrow V'\\longrightarrow L'\\longrightarrow0\n\\]\nwith \\(K',L'\\) line bundles, multiplication gives\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow K'\\otimes\\operatorname{Sym}^{r-1}V'\n\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Sym}^rV'\n\\longrightarrow(L')^r\\longrightarrow0,\n\\qquad r\\ge1.\n\\]\nIndeed, a local splitting gives a kernel generator \\(k'\\) and a lift \\(e'\\) of a quotient generator. The monomials containing \\(k'\\) form the kernel of the symmetric quotient, and the remaining monomial \\((e')^r\\) maps to a quotient generator.</p><p>Apply this construction first to \\(V\\to\\widetilde L_2\\) and then to \\(V\\to\\widetilde L_1\\). For \\(r\\ge2\\), it gives\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow K_1\\otimes\\operatorname{Sym}^{r-1}V\n\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Sym}^rV\n\\longrightarrow\\widetilde L_2^r\\longrightarrow0,\n\\]\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow K\\otimes\\operatorname{Sym}^{r-2}V\n\\longrightarrow K_1\\otimes\\operatorname{Sym}^{r-1}V\n\\longrightarrow K_1\\otimes\\widetilde L_1^{r-1}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThus each pair of steps leaves the residual bundle\n\\[\nK\\otimes\\operatorname{Sym}^{r-2}V,\n\\]\nwith one line-bundle quotient at each step.</p><p>After \\(j\\) pairs, the residual bundle is\n\\[\nK^j\\otimes\\operatorname{Sym}^{n-2j}V,\n\\]\nwhose second quotient is\n\\[\nK^jK_1\\widetilde L_1^{n-2j-1}\n=K^j\\widetilde L_1^{n-2j}(-\\widetilde D).\n\\]\nIteration gives the asserted factors. Only the second quotient contains an additional twist by \\(-\\widetilde D\\) beyond the factors absorbed into \\(K^j\\).</p><p>Each step uses just one of the locally split quotient sequences, so it remains valid when the two kernel lines have the same fiber image. Concretely, in the pulled-back local basis\n\\[\ne=(1,h),\\qquad f=(0,d)\n\\]\nfrom Proposition \\(1\\), the kernels are generated by\n\\[\nk=de-hf\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad f,\n\\]\nwith \\(h\\) a unit. For \\(n=2\\), the filtration is\n\\[\n\\mathcal O(kf)\\subset kV\\subset\\operatorname{Sym}^2V.\n\\]\nSince \\(e,k\\) are also a basis of \\(V\\), the module \\(kV\\) has basis \\(ke,k^2\\) and is a direct summand of \\(\\operatorname{Sym}^2V\\), with complement generated by \\(e^2\\). Moreover,\n\\[\nkf=\\frac d h\\,ke-\\frac1h\\,k^2.\n\\]\nThe coefficient \\(-1/h\\) is a unit, so \\(kf\\) extends to a basis of \\(kV\\). Both inclusions therefore have locally free quotients. This verification uses no smoothness of \\(d=0\\) and applies at nodes and to nonreduced pullbacks of the divisor.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "This is the global-generation step in the proof of original Proposition \\(3.1\\).", "statement": "<p>This is the global-generation step in the proof of original Proposition \\(3.1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(V,K,\\widetilde L_i,\\widetilde D\\) as in Lemma \\(2.1\\). Let \\(n\\) be even and satisfy \\(n-1\\ge3m\\), and set\n\\[\nW=\\operatorname{Sym}^nV\\otimes P^{n-1}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The bundle \\(W\\) is globally generated.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemma \\(2.1\\), the bidegrees of the filtration factors of \\(W\\) are\n\\[\n(18mj+n-1,\\;18mj+24m(n-2j)+n-1),\n\\]\n\\[\n(18mj+24m(n-2j)-3m+n-1,\\;18mj-3m+n-1),\n\\qquad 0\\le j&lt;n/2,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(9mn+n-1,\\;9mn+n-1).\n\\]\nEvery entry is nonnegative: the only possibly negative contribution is \\(-3m\\), and it is offset by \\(n-1\\).</p><p>For \\(c,t\\ge0\\), products of homogeneous monomials generate \\(\\mathcal O_S(c,t)\\). Moreover,\n\\[\nH^1(\\mathbf P^1,\\mathcal O(c))\n=H^1(\\mathbf P^1,\\mathcal O(t))=0,\n\\]\nas computed on the two standard affine charts, so the Künneth formula gives\n\\[\nH^1(S,\\mathcal O_S(c,t))=0.\n\\]</p><p>If\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow U\\longrightarrow Z\\longrightarrow Q\\longrightarrow0\n\\]\nis an extension of globally generated bundles with\n\\[\nH^1(S,U)=H^1(S,Q)=0,\n\\]\nthe cohomology sequence gives \\(H^1(S,Z)=0\\) and lifts every global section of \\(Q\\) to \\(Z\\). The lifted generators of \\(Q\\), together with generators of \\(U\\), span each fiber of \\(Z\\).</p><p>Starting with the innermost line factor and applying this observation successively proves that \\(W\\) is globally generated.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "This is the projective-embedding step in the proof of original Proposition \\(3.1\\).", "statement": "<p>This is the projective-embedding step in the proof of original Proposition \\(3.1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(m\\ge1\\), let \\(V=f_m^*G\\), and let \\(n\\) be even with \\(n-1\\ge3m\\). Set\n\\[\nW=\\operatorname{Sym}^nV\\otimes P^{n-1},\n\\qquad\nH=H^0(S,W),\n\\]\nand let \\(\\pi:\\mathbf P(V)\\to S\\) be the bundle projection.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The line bundle\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P(V)}(1)\\otimes\\pi^*P\\bigr)^n\n\\]\nis very ample.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemma \\(2.2\\), evaluation gives a surjection\n\\[\nH\\otimes\\mathcal O_S\\longrightarrow W.\n\\]\nIt induces a closed immersion\n\\[\n\\mathbf P(W)\\hookrightarrow\\mathbf P(H)\\times S\n\\]\nwhose projective hyperplane line pulls back to \\(\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P(W)}(1)\\).</p><p>The relative Veronese closed immersion\n\\[\n\\mathbf P(V)\\longrightarrow\\mathbf P(\\operatorname{Sym}^nV)\n\\]\nsends a quotient line \\(\\ell\\) to \\(\\ell^{\\otimes n}\\). Under the natural identification\n\\[\n\\mathbf P(\\operatorname{Sym}^nV)\\simeq\\mathbf P(W),\n\\]\nits tautological pullback is\n\\[\n\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P(V)}(n)\\otimes\\pi^*P^{n-1}.\n\\]\nConsequently their composition embeds \\(\\mathbf P(V)\\) into \\(\\mathbf P(H)\\times S\\) with this hyperplane pullback from the first factor.</p><p>Embedding \\(S\\) by \\(P=\\mathcal O_S(1,1)\\) and then applying the Segre embedding gives a closed immersion into projective space whose hyperplane line pulls back to\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P(V)}(n)\\otimes\\pi^*P^{n-1}\\bigr)\\otimes\\pi^*P\n=\n\\bigl(\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P(V)}(1)\\otimes\\pi^*P\\bigr)^n.\n\\]\nThis line is therefore very ample.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition \\(4.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Unlike Propositions \\(1\\) and \\(2\\), let \\(G\\) now be an arbitrary holomorphic vector bundle of positive rank on\n  \\[\n  S=\\mathbf P^1\\times\\mathbf P^1,\n  \\]\n  and put \\(F=G^\\vee\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P=\\mathcal O_S(1,1)\\), and let \\(f_m:S\\to S\\) be the coordinatewise \\(m\\)-th power map.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that \\(f_m^*G\\otimes P\\) admits a smooth strictly Griffiths-positive Hermitian metric for infinitely many positive integers \\(m\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a function\n\\[\nq:F\\longrightarrow[0,\\infty)\n\\]\nwhich is plurisubharmonic on the total space, is not identically zero, and has the following property: for every \\(x\\in S\\), there is a finite positive semidefinite Hermitian form \\(H_x\\) on \\(F_x\\) such that\n\\[\nq(x,v)=H_x(v,v)\\qquad(v\\in F_x).\n\\]\nIn particular, this fiberwise assertion holds at every base point.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose an unbounded sequence of integers for which \\(f_m^*G\\otimes P\\) has a smooth strictly Griffiths-positive Hermitian metric.</p><p>Lemma \\(3.1\\) constructs normalized trace quadratics on \\(F\\). Lemma \\(3.2\\) extracts a measurable positive semidefinite coefficient limit with the untwisted transition law, and Lemma \\(3.3\\) proves that this measurable form is nonzero. Lemma \\(3.4\\) produces its finite, nonnegative, globally plurisubharmonic representative \\(q\\), which is a Hermitian quadratic on every fiber.</p><p>Since the coefficients of \\(q\\) represent the original measurable form almost everywhere, the nonvanishing established in Lemma \\(3.3\\) implies \\(q\\not\\equiv0\\).</p><p>As noted in original Remark \\(4.2\\), the function represents a distributional limit; no convergence of restrictions to a divisor is asserted. The graph identities will instead be evaluated directly in this single everywhere-defined function \\(q\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "This is the trace-and-normalization step in the proof of original Proposition \\(4.1\\).", "statement": "<p>This is the trace-and-normalization step in the proof of original Proposition \\(4.1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the assumptions of Proposition \\(3\\), put \\(r=\\operatorname{rank}G\\), and choose an unbounded sequence of the indicated integers \\(m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_m\\) denote the smooth dual metric on\n  \\[\n  (f_m^*G\\otimes P)^\\vee=f_m^*F\\otimes P^{-1}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix, for each \\(m\\), the holomorphic isomorphism\n  \\[\n  f_m^*P^{-1}\\simeq P^{-m}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(t\\) is a nonvanishing holomorphic frame of \\(P^{-1}\\) on \\(U\\subset S\\), define\n  \\[\n  q_{m,t}(x,v)=\\frac1{m^2}\n  \\sum_{y\\in f_m^{-1}(x)}\n  \\left\\|v\\otimes(f_m^*t)^{1/m}(y)\\right\\|_{m,y}^2,\n  \\]\n  counting multiplicities.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose smooth background Hermitian norms \\(|\\cdot|_0\\) on \\(F\\) and \\(P^{-1}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The functions \\(q_{m,t}\\) are well defined, continuous, jointly plurisubharmonic on \\(F|_U\\), and positive definite Hermitian quadratics in \\(v\\). For a frame change \\(t'=gt\\),\n\\[\nq_{m,t'}(x,v)=|g(x)|^{2/m}q_{m,t}(x,v).\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nN_m(x,v)=q_{m,t}(x,v)|t(x)|_0^{-2/m}\n\\]\nis a globally defined continuous quadratic. Rescaling the dual metric by a positive scalar, one may arrange\n\\[\n\\max_{x\\in S,\\ |v|_0=1}N_m(x,v)=1.\n\\]\nAfter this normalization,\n\\[\n0\\le q_{m,t}(x,v)\\le |t(x)|_0^{2/m}|v|_0^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The squared dual norm is plurisubharmonic on the total space. We recall the sign in this standard implication. For a smooth Hermitian metric with matrix \\(h\\), choose a holomorphic frame normal at the point in question, so that \\(h=\\mathrm{Id}\\) and its first derivatives vanish there. Along a holomorphic disk with coordinate \\(\\zeta\\), write the Hermitian curvature as\n\\[\niC\\,d\\zeta\\wedge d\\bar\\zeta.\n\\]\nIn the convention used here,\n\\[\nC=-\\frac{\\partial^2h}{\\partial\\zeta\\,\\partial\\bar\\zeta}\n\\]\nat the normal point. Thus, for a holomorphic section \\(s\\) along the disk,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial\\zeta\\,\\partial\\bar\\zeta}\\|s\\|^2\n=\n\\|D'_{\\partial/\\partial\\zeta}s\\|^2-\\langle Cs,s\\rangle.\n\\]\nDuality reverses Griffiths positivity, so the second term on the right is nonnegative for the dual metric. Applying this calculation to the section of the pulled-back bundle defined by any disk in its total space proves the assertion.</p><p>The root in the definition of \\(q_{m,t}\\) is local on the source. Indeed, in a local frame \\(s\\) of \\(P^{-1}\\), the section \\(f_m^*t\\) has the form\n\\[\n\\eta(y)s^m\n\\]\nwith \\(\\eta\\) holomorphic and nowhere zero. A local holomorphic logarithm of \\(\\eta\\) gives the root\n\\[\n\\exp((\\log\\eta)/m)s.\n\\]\nTwo choices differ by an \\(m\\)-th root of unity, which does not change the squared norm. The trace is consequently well defined and is a positive definite Hermitian quadratic in \\(v\\).</p><p>We verify its regularity through the branch locus. Off that locus, every local inverse branch of \\(f_m\\) gives a holomorphic map\n\\[\n(x,v)\\longmapsto\n\\left(y(x),v\\otimes(f_m^*t)^{1/m}(y(x))\\right)\n\\]\ninto \\(f_m^*F\\otimes P^{-1}\\). Hence each summand, and their sum, is plurisubharmonic.</p><p>The unordered multiset of roots of \\(y^m=x\\) varies continuously with \\(x\\), counting coalescing roots with their multiplicities. Applied in the two factors, and using local source frames for the norm in the trace, this proves continuity of the sum across the branch locus, including a crossing of its two components.</p><p>The continuous trace is plurisubharmonic across the branch locus as well. In a local total-space chart, the inverse image of the branch locus under \\(F|_U\\to U\\) is an analytic set, contained in\n\\[\n\\{z_1z_2=0\\}\n\\]\nafter choosing affine coordinates on the two base factors. It is therefore pluripolar. The trace is locally bounded above there by continuity, so the removable-singularities theorem for plurisubharmonic functions gives a unique plurisubharmonic extension. Its upper-limit values agree with the continuous trace. This proves joint plurisubharmonicity on all of \\(F|_U\\).</p><p>If \\(t'=gt\\), then\n\\[\nq_{m,t'}(x,v)=|g(x)|^{2/m}q_{m,t}(x,v).\n\\]\nSince \\(|t'|_0=|g||t|_0\\), the expression defining \\(N_m\\) is globally defined.</p><p>The background unit sphere bundle is compact, and \\(N_m\\) is positive there. Multiplying the dual metric’s squared norm by a positive constant, we may therefore arrange\n\\[\n\\max_{x\\in S,\\ |v|_0=1}N_m(x,v)=1.\n\\]\nThis rescaling does not change the curvature. Homogeneity gives\n\\[\n0\\le q_{m,t}(x,v)\\le |t(x)|_0^{2/m}|v|_0^2.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "This is the measurable-coefficient-limit step in the proof of original Proposition \\(4.1\\).", "statement": "<p>This is the measurable-coefficient-limit step in the proof of original Proposition \\(4.1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(q_{m,t}\\) be the normalized trace functions of Lemma \\(3.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In a holomorphic trivialization of \\(F|_U\\), write\n  \\[\n  q_{m,t}(x,v)=v^*H_m(x)v.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After passing to a subsequence on a countable collection of charts and compact exhaustions, the coefficient matrices \\(H_m\\) have distributional limits \\(H\\). These limits belong to \\(L^\\infty_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\), are Hermitian, and are positive semidefinite almost everywhere.</p><p>They have the untwisted transition law of a measurable Hermitian form on \\(F\\): if \\(v=T(x)w\\) is a holomorphic change of bundle coordinates, the corresponding limits satisfy\n\\[\nH'=T^*HT\n\\]\nalmost everywhere.</p>", "proof": "<p>On each relatively compact subset of \\(U\\), the positive matrices \\(H_m\\) are uniformly bounded by the estimate in Lemma \\(3.1\\). Weak compactness in \\(L^2\\) and a diagonal subsequence on a countable collection of charts and compact exhaustions give distributional limits \\(H\\) for all the coefficient matrices.</p><p>These limits belong to \\(L^\\infty_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\), are Hermitian, and are positive semidefinite almost everywhere. For example, positivity and the same local upper bound pass to \\(c^*Hc\\) against every nonnegative test function for each\n\\[\nc\\in(\\mathbf Q+i\\mathbf Q)^r;\n\\]\ndensity then gives the matrix inequalities on a single set of full measure.</p><p>To check the transition law, let \\(v=T(x)w\\) be a holomorphic change of bundle coordinates and let \\(t'=gt\\). The original matrices satisfy\n\\[\nH_m'=|g|^{2/m}T^*H_mT.\n\\]\nOn a compact subset \\(K\\) of the overlap, the error obtained by deleting the scalar factor has, against a smooth compactly supported matrix test function \\(\\Phi\\), the bound\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\left\\langle\n(|g|^{2/m}-1)T^*H_mT,\\Phi\n\\right\\rangle\n\\right|\n\\le\nC_K\\|\\Phi\\|_{L^1}\n\\sup_K\\bigl||g|^{2/m}-1\\bigr|\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nHere the local matrix bound and the boundedness of \\(T\\) are absorbed into \\(C_K\\). Multiplication by the fixed smooth matrices \\(T,T^*\\) preserves distributional convergence, as does a change of base coordinates. Consequently,\n\\[\nH'=T^*HT\n\\]\nalmost everywhere.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "This is the nonvanishing step in the proof of original Proposition \\(4.1\\).", "statement": "<p>This is the nonvanishing step in the proof of original Proposition \\(4.1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(H\\) be the measurable coefficient limit obtained in Lemma \\(3.2\\) from the trace functions normalized as in Lemma \\(3.1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The measurable Hermitian form represented by \\(H\\) is not zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose maximizing unit vectors \\((x_m,v_m)\\) in\n\\[\n\\max_{x\\in S,\\ |v|_0=1}N_m(x,v)=1.\n\\]\nAfter a further subsequence they converge in the compact unit sphere bundle to \\((x_*,v_*)\\). This further subsequence retains all the distributional limits already obtained.</p><p>In one fixed coordinate and frame chart around \\(x_*\\), let \\(c_m\\to c\\) be their fiber coordinates. Then \\(c\\ne0\\), and a local operator bound \\(\\|H_m\\|\\le C\\) gives\n\\[\n\\left|\nc^*H_m(x_m)c-c_m^*H_m(x_m)c_m\n\\right|\n\\le C|c-c_m|(|c|+|c_m|)\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nOn the other hand,\n\\[\nc_m^*H_m(x_m)c_m=|t(x_m)|_0^{2/m}\\longrightarrow1,\n\\]\nsince the norm of the nonvanishing frame is bounded above and away from zero on a fixed compact neighborhood.</p><p>Thus the nonnegative plurisubharmonic function\n\\[\nu_m(x)=c^*H_m(x)c\n\\]\nsatisfies \\(u_m(x_m)\\ge\\frac12\\) for large \\(m\\).</p><p>Choose \\(\\varrho&gt;0\\) so that \\(B(x_*,4\\varrho)\\) lies in the coordinate chart, and choose a fixed smooth nonnegative function \\(\\chi\\) supported in \\(B(x_*,3\\varrho)\\) and equal to one on \\(B(x_*,2\\varrho)\\). For all sufficiently large \\(m\\), the ball \\(B(x_m,\\varrho)\\) lies in that latter ball. The submean inequality yields\n\\[\n\\int\\chi(x)c^*H_m(x)c\\,dx\n\\ge\n\\int_{B(x_m,\\varrho)}u_m(x)\\,dx\n\\ge\n\\frac12\\operatorname{Vol}(B(0,\\varrho)).\n\\]\nThe left side is a pairing against the fixed smooth matrix test function \\(\\chi cc^*\\), so the inequality passes to \\(H\\). It proves that \\(H\\) is nonzero as a measurable form.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "This is the everywhere-quadratic-representative step in the proof of original Proposition \\(4.1\\).", "statement": "<p>This is the everywhere-quadratic-representative step in the proof of original Proposition \\(4.1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be the local \\(L^\\infty_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\) coefficient limits obtained in Lemma \\(3.2\\), with\n  \\[\n  H'=T^*HT\n  \\]\n  almost everywhere on overlaps.</p><p class=\"li\">These limits arise from the locally uniformly bounded, jointly plurisubharmonic Hermitian quadratics \\(q_{m,t}(x,v)=v^*H_m(x)v\\) of Lemma \\(3.1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The measurable forms \\(H\\) have a globally defined representative\n\\[\nq:F\\to[0,\\infty)\n\\]\nwhich is finite and plurisubharmonic on the total space and is a positive semidefinite Hermitian quadratic on every fiber. Its local coefficient matrices equal \\(H\\) almost everywhere.</p>", "proof": "<p>The measurable form is defined only up to a set of measure zero in the base. Even a pointwise limit of the traces may fail to be plurisubharmonic: in one base variable, tracing\n\\[\n(1+|y|^2)|v|^2\n\\]\nthrough \\(x=y^m\\) gives\n\\[\n(1+|x|^{2/m})|v|^2.\n\\]\nIts pointwise limit has coefficient \\(1\\) at \\(x=0\\) and \\(2\\) elsewhere, whereas its plurisubharmonic representative has coefficient \\(2\\) everywhere. We recover the intrinsic plurisubharmonic representative of the measurable form and show that it remains a Hermitian quadratic on every fiber.</p><p>Work in a coordinate and bundle chart \\(U\\), and let \\(\\rho\\) be a nonnegative smooth radial function on \\(\\mathbf C^2\\), supported in the unit ball and of integral one. Put\n\\[\n\\rho_\\delta(y)=\\delta^{-4}\\rho(y/\\delta),\n\\qquad\nH_\\delta=H*\\rho_\\delta,\n\\qquad\nq_\\delta(x,v)=v^*H_\\delta(x)v\n\\]\non the interior domain where this convolution is defined.</p><p>Convolving \\(H_m\\) in the same way gives\n\\[\nq_{m,\\delta}(x,v)\n=\\int q_{m,t}(x-y,v)\\rho_\\delta(y)\\,dy.\n\\]\nIt is plurisubharmonic jointly in \\((x,v)\\), since each base translation\n\\[\n(x,v)\\longmapsto(x-y,v)\n\\]\nis holomorphic and the average has nonnegative weights.</p><p>For fixed \\(\\delta\\), distributional convergence of \\(H_m\\) implies convergence of the convolved coefficients and all their derivatives at every interior point: these are pairings against translates of derivatives of the fixed smooth kernel. The Levi matrices of \\(q_{m,\\delta}\\) therefore converge pointwise to that of \\(q_\\delta\\). Hence \\(q_\\delta\\) is smooth and plurisubharmonic, and on each smaller chart\n\\[\n0\\le q_\\delta(x,v)\\le C|v|^2\n\\]\nfor all sufficiently small \\(\\delta\\).</p><p>These quadratics decrease as \\(\\delta\\) decreases. To check this for any fixed nonnegative radial kernel, write \\(\\rho(y)=\\varphi(|y|)\\), and let \\(M_u(x,s)\\) denote the spherical mean of a subharmonic function \\(u\\) on \\(\\mathbf R^4\\). Polar coordinates give\n\\[\n(u*\\rho_\\delta)(x)\n=\n|S^3|\\int_0^1\nM_u(x,\\delta s)\\varphi(s)s^3\\,ds.\n\\]\nThe spherical mean is nondecreasing in its radius. The integration weight is nonnegative and does not depend on \\(\\delta\\), so the convolution is nondecreasing in \\(\\delta\\). Apply this to\n\\[\nu(x)=q_{m,t}(x,v)\n\\]\nfor each constant \\(v\\), then pass to the limit in \\(m\\) at two fixed radii. It follows that\n\\[\nH_{\\delta_1}(x)\\le H_{\\delta_2}(x),\n\\qquad 0&lt;\\delta_1&lt;\\delta_2,\n\\]\nin the order of Hermitian forms.</p><p>Thus \\(q_\\delta\\) decreases, as \\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\), to a finite nonnegative plurisubharmonic function \\(q_U\\) on the total-space chart. The decreasing-limit assertion follows also by passing to the submean inequalities. Finiteness is supplied by the local bound.</p><p>At every fixed base point \\(x\\), this limit is a Hermitian quadratic. Indeed, all entries of \\(H_\\delta(x)\\) are determined by the finite set of values at \\(e_j,e_j+e_k,e_j+ie_k\\), through\n\\[\n2\\operatorname{Re}(H_\\delta)_{jk}\n=q_\\delta(x,e_j+e_k)-q_\\delta(x,e_j)-q_\\delta(x,e_k),\n\\]\n\\[\n2\\operatorname{Im}(H_\\delta)_{jk}\n=q_\\delta(x,e_j)+q_\\delta(x,e_k)-q_\\delta(x,e_j+ie_k).\n\\]\nEach value on the right has a finite limit. Hence the entries converge to one Hermitian matrix \\(\\widehat H_U(x)\\), and passage to the finite quadratic polynomial gives\n\\[\nq_U(x,v)=v^*\\widehat H_U(x)v\n\\qquad\\text{for every }v\\in\\mathbf C^r.\n\\]\nThis matrix is positive semidefinite at every \\(x\\), by nonnegativity. At almost every \\(x\\), approximate-identity convergence also gives\n\\[\n\\widehat H_U(x)=H(x).\n\\]</p><p>Finally, these local representatives agree on overlaps. After the holomorphic change of bundle coordinates, their measurable matrices agree outside a base-null set by Lemma \\(3.2\\). The quadratic functions therefore agree for every fiber vector outside that set, hence almost everywhere in the total-space chart by Fubini’s theorem.</p><p>Both functions are locally bounded and plurisubharmonic. Such a function is determined everywhere by its almost-everywhere values: the submean inequality and upper semicontinuity give, in local Euclidean coordinates,\n\\[\nu(p)=\n\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}\n\\frac1{\\operatorname{Vol}(B(p,\\varepsilon))}\n\\int_{B(p,\\varepsilon)}u.\n\\]\nThus the local \\(q_U\\) glue to a single function \\(q\\) on \\(F\\), with all the required pointwise and plurisubharmonic properties.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Proposition \\(5.4\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Return to the graph-defined bundle of Proposition \\(1\\). Thus\n  \\[\n  S=\\mathbf P^1\\times\\mathbf P^1,\\qquad b=24,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  A=\\begin{pmatrix}a&amp;0\\\\0&amp;a^{-1}\\end{pmatrix},\n  \\qquad\n  B=\\begin{pmatrix}s&amp;1\\\\\\sqrt2/3&amp;1-s\\end{pmatrix},\n  \\]\n  where\n  \\[\n  a=e^{i\\pi/3},\\qquad\n  s=\\frac12-\\frac{i(1+2\\sqrt2)}{2\\sqrt3}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\Gamma_M=\\{(x,Mx):x\\in\\mathbf P^1\\},\n  \\qquad\n  D=\\Gamma_I\\cup\\Gamma_A\\cup\\Gamma_B,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  L_1=p_1^*\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P^1}(b),\n  \\qquad\n  L_2=p_2^*\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P^1}(b).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\alpha:L_1^{-1}|_D\\xrightarrow{\\sim}L_2^{-1}|_D,\n  \\qquad\n  \\alpha|_{\\Gamma_M}=M^{\\otimes b},\n  \\]\n  and let\n  \\[\n  G=\\{(u_1,u_2)\\in L_1\\oplus L_2:\n  u_2|_D=(\\alpha^\\vee)^{-1}(u_1|_D)\\},\n  \\qquad F=G^\\vee.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">By Proposition \\(1\\), \\(F\\) has line subbundles\n  \\[\n  \\iota_i:L_i^{-1}\\hookrightarrow F\n  \\]\n  spanning away from \\(D\\) and satisfying \\(\\iota_2\\alpha=\\iota_1\\) on \\(D\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every finite nonnegative plurisubharmonic function on the total space of \\(F\\) whose restriction to each fiber is a Hermitian quadratic form is identically zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>If such a function \\(q\\) were nonzero, Lemma \\(4.1\\) would give a nonzero squared seminorm on \\(\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P^1}(-b)\\) invariant under the lifts of \\(A,B\\).</p><p>Lemma \\(4.2\\) would then produce a finite positive measure on \\(\\mathbf P^1\\) of mass\n\\[\n2\\pi b&gt;0,\n\\]\ninvariant under both projective transformations. This contradicts Lemma \\(4.3\\). Therefore \\(q\\) is identically zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(5.1\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the graph-defined bundle \\(G\\), its dual \\(F\\), and the inclusions \\(\\iota_i\\) of Proposition \\(4\\). Suppose\n\\[\nq:F\\longrightarrow[0,\\infty)\n\\]\nis finite, plurisubharmonic, not identically zero, and a Hermitian quadratic form on every fiber.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a finite, nonnegative, nonzero plurisubharmonic function \\(a_\\bullet\\) on \\(\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P^1}(-b)\\), a positive semidefinite Hermitian quadratic on each fiber, such that\n\\[\na_\\bullet(Mx,M^{\\otimes b}v)=a_\\bullet(x,v),\n\\qquad M=A,B,\n\\]\nfor every \\(x\\in\\mathbf P^1\\) and \\(v\\in\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P^1}(-b)_x\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write\n\\[\nq(x,v)=H_x(v,v);\n\\]\nnonnegativity says that every \\(H_x\\) is positive semidefinite. Pull \\(q\\) back by\n\\[\n\\iota_i:L_i^{-1}=p_i^*\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P^1}(-b)\\longrightarrow F,\n\\qquad i=1,2.\n\\]</p><p>For fixed \\(x_i\\in\\mathbf P^1\\) and \\(v\\in\\mathcal O(-b)_{x_i}\\), the function obtained by holding \\(v\\) fixed while varying the other base coordinate is finite and subharmonic on the complementary \\(\\mathbf P^1\\). It is constant by the maximum principle.</p><p>Consequently, \\(q\\circ\\iota_i\\) descends to a finite nonnegative squared seminorm \\(a_i\\) on \\(\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P^1}(-b)\\) over the \\(i\\)-th factor. Fixing any point in the other factor realizes \\(a_i\\) as a holomorphic pullback of \\(q\\), so \\(a_i\\) is plurisubharmonic on the total line bundle.</p><p>On \\(\\Gamma_M\\), the identity \\(\\iota_2\\alpha=\\iota_1\\) and the definition of \\(\\alpha\\) give\n\\[\na_1(x,v)=a_2(Mx,M^{\\otimes b}v),\n\\qquad M\\in\\{\\mathrm{Id},A,B\\}.\n\\]\nThe case \\(M=\\mathrm{Id}\\) identifies \\(a_1,a_2\\) as a single function \\(a_\\bullet\\) on \\(\\mathcal O(-b)\\).</p><p>This function is not identically zero. Indeed, if it were zero, then for every \\(x\\notin D\\), the form \\(H_x\\) would vanish on each of two spanning lines. The inequality\n\\[\n|H_x(v,w)|^2\\le H_x(v,v)H_x(w,w)\n\\]\nwould then force \\(H_x=0\\). Hence \\(q\\) would vanish on the full-measure complement of the inverse image of \\(D\\) in \\(F\\). Nonnegativity and the submean inequality on small total-space coordinate balls would give \\(q=0\\) everywhere, a contradiction.</p><p>The cases \\(M=A,B\\) give the asserted invariances under the holomorphic lifts \\(A^{\\otimes b},B^{\\otimes b}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(5.2\\), “Logarithmic curvature of a squared seminorm.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For this lemma, replace the particular line bundle and base in Proposition \\(4\\) by a holomorphic line bundle \\(L\\) on an arbitrary connected Riemann surface \\(C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  a_\\bullet:L\\longrightarrow[0,\\infty)\n  \\]\n  is finite, plurisubharmonic, and not identically zero, and its restriction to every fiber is a positive semidefinite Hermitian quadratic form.</p><p class=\"li\">In a nonvanishing holomorphic frame \\(e\\) on \\(U\\subset C\\), write\n  \\[\n  a_\\bullet(z,we(z))=u_e(z)|w|^2.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The function \\(\\log u_e\\), with the value \\(-\\infty\\) allowed, is subharmonic and locally integrable. The local measures\n\\[\ni\\partial\\bar\\partial\\log u_e\n\\]\ndefine a positive Radon measure \\(\\mu\\) on \\(C\\).</p><p>If a holomorphic bundle automorphism \\(\\widetilde T:L\\to L\\) covers an automorphism \\(T:C\\to C\\) and satisfies\n\\[\na_\\bullet\\circ\\widetilde T=a_\\bullet,\n\\]\nthen \\(T^*\\mu=\\mu\\).</p><p>If \\(C=\\mathbf P^1\\) and \\(L=\\mathcal O_{\\mathbf P^1}(-b)\\) with \\(b\\ge1\\), then\n\\[\n\\mu(\\mathbf P^1)=2\\pi b.\n\\]\nWhere \\(u_e\\) is smooth and strictly positive, \\(i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\log u_e\\) is the negative of the Chern curvature of the corresponding metric in the convention used here.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a coordinate disk \\(U\\) and a frame \\(e\\), and abbreviate \\(u_e\\) to \\(u\\). The function \\(u\\) is finite, nonnegative, subharmonic, and locally bounded, by restriction to the holomorphic section \\(e\\).</p><p>Choose a nonnegative smooth radial function \\(\\rho\\) supported in the unit disk, with integral one, and put\n\\[\n\\rho_\\delta(z)=\\delta^{-2}\\rho(z/\\delta),\n\\qquad\nu_\\delta=u*\\rho_\\delta+\\delta.\n\\]\nThese functions are defined on the interior of \\(U\\) for sufficiently small \\(\\delta&gt;0\\).</p><p>Averaging the holomorphic base translations\n\\[\n(z,w)\\longmapsto(z-\\zeta,w)\n\\]\nshows that \\((u*\\rho_\\delta)(z)|w|^2\\) is plurisubharmonic. Adding \\(\\delta|w|^2\\) preserves this property, so \\(u_\\delta&gt;0\\) and \\(u_\\delta(z)|w|^2\\) is smooth and plurisubharmonic. Its Levi matrix at \\(w=1\\) is\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n(u_\\delta)_{z\\bar z}&amp;(u_\\delta)_z\\\\\n(u_\\delta)_{\\bar z}&amp;u_\\delta\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nIts nonnegative determinant gives\n\\[\n(\\log u_\\delta)_{z\\bar z}\n=\n\\frac{u_\\delta(u_\\delta)_{z\\bar z}-|(u_\\delta)_z|^2}\n{u_\\delta^2}\n\\ge0.\n\\]\nThus \\(f_\\delta:=\\log u_\\delta\\) is subharmonic.</p><p>The radial convolutions are nonnegative weighted averages of circular means of \\(u\\). These means increase with their radii and converge to \\(u\\) as the radii tend to zero. Consequently,\n\\[\nu_\\delta\\downarrow u,\\qquad\nf_\\delta\\downarrow\\log u\n\\quad\\text{as }\\delta\\downarrow0.\n\\]</p><p>We establish local integrability before taking distributional derivatives of the limit. Suppose \\(u(z_0)&gt;0\\), and choose \\(R&gt;0\\) with\n\\[\n\\overline{B(z_0,R)}\\subset U.\n\\]\nFor all sufficiently small \\(\\delta\\), local boundedness supplies \\(C_0\\ge0\\) such that \\(f_\\delta\\le C_0\\) on this disk. Moreover,\n\\[\nf_\\delta(z_0)\\ge\\log u(z_0).\n\\]\nIntegrating the circular submean inequality over the radius gives\n\\[\n\\int_{B(z_0,R)}f_\\delta\\,dA\n\\ge\\pi R^2\\log u(z_0).\n\\]\nWriting \\(f^-=\\max\\{-f,0\\}\\), we obtain\n\\[\n\\int_{B(z_0,R)}f_\\delta^-\\,dA\n\\le\\pi R^2\\bigl(C_0-\\log u(z_0)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe negative parts increase as \\(\\delta\\) decreases, whereas the positive parts are bounded by \\(C_0\\). Monotone convergence therefore proves\n\\[\n\\log u\\in L^1(B(z_0,R)).\n\\]\nIt also gives \\(f_\\delta\\to\\log u\\) in \\(L^1\\) on this disk.</p><p>This conclusion propagates throughout \\(C\\). Let \\(\\Omega\\) consist of the points near which a local logarithmic weight is integrable. A frame change \\(e'=ge\\) gives\n\\[\n\\log u_{e'}=\\log u_e+\\log|g|^2,\n\\]\nso \\(\\Omega\\) is independent of the chosen frame.</p><p>It is open and nonempty: nontriviality of \\(a_\\bullet\\) supplies a point with \\(u&gt;0\\), to which the preceding estimate applies. If \\(x\\) is in the boundary of \\(\\Omega\\), choose a coordinate disk containing \\(\\overline{B(x,4r)}\\). There is a small open disk contained in \\(\\Omega\\cap B(x,r)\\). On that disk, integrability of \\(\\log u\\) implies \\(u&gt;0\\) almost everywhere, so it contains a point \\(z_0\\) with \\(u(z_0)&gt;0\\).</p><p>The disk \\(B(z_0,2r)\\) has closure in the original chart and contains a neighborhood of \\(x\\). Applying the same estimate on this larger disk proves \\(x\\in\\Omega\\). Thus \\(\\Omega\\) is also closed, and connectedness gives \\(\\Omega=C\\).</p><p>On every relatively compact disk, \\(C_0-f_\\delta\\) increases to the integrable function \\(C_0-\\log u\\). Hence the local convergence is in \\(L^1\\), and the decreasing limit \\(\\log u\\) is subharmonic. In particular,\n\\[\ni\\partial\\bar\\partial\\log u\n\\]\nis a positive distribution and thus a positive Radon measure. Since \\(g\\) in the frame-change formula is holomorphic and nowhere zero, \\(\\log|g|^2\\) is harmonic. The local measures therefore glue to \\(\\mu\\).</p><p>For invariance, choose frames \\(e\\) on \\(U\\), \\(e'\\) on \\(V\\), with \\(T(U)\\subset V\\), and write\n\\[\n\\widetilde T(e(z))=c(z)e'(Tz),\n\\]\nwhere \\(c\\) is holomorphic and nowhere zero. The isometry condition gives\n\\[\nu_e(z)=|c(z)|^2u_{e'}(Tz),\n\\qquad\n\\log u_e=\\log|c|^2+(\\log u_{e'})\\circ T.\n\\]\nTaking distributional \\(i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\), which commutes with pullback by the holomorphic diffeomorphism \\(T\\), gives\n\\[\n\\mu=T^*\\mu.\n\\]\nHere pullback is that of a top-degree current: for a test function \\(\\varphi\\),\n\\[\n\\int_C\\varphi\\,d(T^*\\mu)\n=\n\\int_C(\\varphi\\circ T^{-1})\\,d\\mu.\n\\]\nEquivalently,\n\\[\n(T^*\\mu)(E)=\\mu(T(E))\n\\]\nfor Borel sets \\(E\\). This is ordinary measure invariance under the bijection \\(T\\); in particular,\n\\[\nT^*\\delta_p=\\delta_{T^{-1}p}\n\\]\nwith unchanged atom mass.</p><p>Finally, suppose \\(C=\\mathbf P^1\\) and \\(L=\\mathcal O(-b)\\). Let \\(h_0\\) be the smooth Hermitian metric on \\(\\mathcal O(-b)\\) induced by the standard norm on \\(\\mathbf C^2\\). In the tautological affine frame\n\\[\ne(z)=(1,z)^{\\otimes b},\n\\]\nits weight is\n\\[\nu_0(z)=h_0(e(z),e(z))=(1+|z|^2)^b.\n\\]\nThe local functions\n\\[\n\\log u_e-\\log h_0(e,e)\n\\]\ndefine a single globally integrable function \\(\\Phi\\) on \\(\\mathbf P^1\\), by the common frame law. If\n\\[\n\\mu_0=i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\log h_0(e,e),\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\mu-\\mu_0=i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\Phi,\n\\qquad\n\\int_{\\mathbf P^1}(\\mu-\\mu_0)\n=\n\\langle i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\Phi,1\\rangle=0.\n\\]\nThe measure \\(\\mu\\) is finite because it is locally finite on the compact sphere. The smooth reference measure has mass\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbf P^1}\\mu_0\n=\n\\int_{\\mathbf C}\n\\frac{b\\,i\\,dz\\wedge d\\bar z}{(1+|z|^2)^2}\n=\n4\\pi b\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{r\\,dr}{(1+r^2)^2}\n=2\\pi b.\n\\]\nThis proves the mass formula. Zeros of \\(u_e\\) and any atoms of \\(\\mu\\) are included in the distributional calculation; no points have been excised.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(5.3\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(A,B\\) be the projective transformations induced by the matrices in Proposition \\(4\\). By Proposition \\(1\\), their projective orders are three, they have no common fixed point, and \\(AB\\) has eigenvalues of distinct moduli.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The transformations \\(A,B\\) preserve no nonzero finite positive Borel measure on \\(\\mathbf P^1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose that \\(\\mu\\) is such a measure. It is invariant under \\(T=AB\\). The two eigenvalues of \\(AB\\) have distinct moduli, so after a projective change of coordinate, \\(T\\) acts as\n\\[\nz\\longmapsto\\lambda z,\n\\qquad R:=|\\lambda|&gt;1.\n\\]\nThe Borel sets\n\\[\nC_n=\\{z\\in\\mathbf C^*:R^n\\le|z|&lt;R^{n+1}\\},\n\\qquad n\\in\\mathbf Z,\n\\]\nare pairwise disjoint, cover \\(\\mathbf C^*\\), and satisfy\n\\[\nT(C_n)=C_{n+1}.\n\\]\nEach has the same measure. Finiteness and positivity imply that this common measure is zero, and countable additivity gives\n\\[\n\\mu(\\mathbf C^*)=0.\n\\]\nThe half-open annuli assign every boundary circle to exactly one set, so this argument applies equally to atomic and diffuse measures and to nonreal \\(\\lambda\\).</p><p>Thus the nonempty support of \\(\\mu\\) consists of one or two of the fixed points of \\(T\\). Invariance under \\(A,B\\) preserves this support as a set: the inverse image of an open neighborhood of the image of a support point has positive measure.</p><p>Both projective transformations have order three. Their induced permutations on a set of at most two points are therefore trivial. Each point of the support would be fixed by both \\(A,B\\), and hence would be a common eigenline of the two matrices. Proposition \\(1\\) excludes such a line.</p>"}], "edges": [[2, 1, "part"], [3, 1, "part"], [5, 4, "part"], [6, 4, "part"], [7, 4, "part"], [9, 8, "part"], [10, 8, "part"], [11, 8, "part"], [12, 8, "part"], [14, 13, "part"], [15, 13, "part"], [16, 13, "part"]]});
